{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Melanoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in melanocytes, the cells that color the skin.","source_span":"Melanoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in melanocytes (cells that color the skin)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing over the last 40 years.","source_span":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing over the last 40 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"When melanoma starts in the skin it is called cutaneous melanoma.","source_span":"When melanoma starts in the skin, it is called cutaneous melanoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"When melanoma occurs in the eye it is called intraocular or ocular melanoma.","source_span":"When melanoma occurs in the eye, it is called intraocular or ocular melanoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"In men, melanoma is often found on the trunk or the head and neck; in women, it forms most often on the arms and legs.","source_span":"In men, melanoma is often found on the trunk (the area from the shoulders to the hips) or the head and neck. In women, melanoma forms most often on the arms and legs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Nonmelanoma skin cancers, the most common types of skin cancer, rarely spread to other parts of the body.","source_span":"Nonmelanoma skin cancers rarely spread to other parts of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Risk factors for melanoma include fair complexion, long-term natural or artificial sunlight exposure, environmental exposures such as radiation, solvents, vinyl chloride and PCBs, a history of many blistering sunburns, having several large or many small moles, a family history of atypical nevus syndrome, a family or personal history of melanoma, being white, a weakened immune system, and certain gene changes.","source_span":"Risk factors for melanoma include the following:...Having a fair complexion...Being exposed to natural sunlight or artificial sunlight...Being exposed to certain factors in the environment...radiation, solvents, vinyl chloride, and PCBs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Signs of possible melanoma include a mole that changes in size, shape, or color, has irregular edges, is more than one color, is asymmetrical, itches, oozes/bleeds/ulcerates, or new satellite moles growing near an existing mole.","source_span":"A mole that: - changes in size, shape, or color. - has irregular edges or borders. - is more than one color. - is asymmetrical...Satellite moles (new moles that grow near an existing mole)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Melanoma is detected and diagnosed using a skin exam and biopsy, in which a pathologist examines removed tissue under a microscope.","source_span":"Skin exam: A doctor or nurse checks the skin for moles...Biopsy: A procedure to remove the abnormal tissue and a small amount of normal tissue around it. A pathologist looks at the tissue under a microscope to check for cancer cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","statement":"Abnormal areas of skin should not be shaved off or cauterized because cancer cells that remain may spread.","source_span":"It is important that abnormal areas of the skin not be shaved off or cauterized (destroyed with a hot instrument, an electric current, or a caustic substance) because cancer cells that remain may"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is melanoma and where does it form in the skin?","answer":"Melanoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in melanocytes, the cells that give the skin its color."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How has the number of new melanoma cases changed over the past several decades?","answer":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing over the last 40 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What are melanomas of the skin and of the eye each called?","answer":"Melanoma that starts in the skin is called cutaneous melanoma, while melanoma that occurs in the eye is called intraocular or ocular melanoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Where on the body does melanoma most commonly occur in men compared to women?","answer":"In men, melanoma is often found on the trunk or the head and neck; in women, it most often forms on the arms and legs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What factors increase a person's risk of developing melanoma?","answer":"Risk factors include a fair complexion, long-term natural or artificial sun exposure, environmental exposures like radiation, solvents, vinyl chloride and PCBs, a history of blistering sunburns, many moles, a family history of atypical nevus syndrome, a personal or family history of melanoma, being white, a weakened immune system, and certain gene changes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What changes in a mole could be a warning sign of melanoma?","answer":"Warning signs include a mole that changes in size, shape, or color, has irregular borders, is more than one color, is asymmetrical, itches, oozes, bleeds, or ulcerates, or the appearance of new satellite moles nearby."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma is a very rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the skin.","source_span":"Merkel cell carcinoma is a very rare disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma is also called neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin or trabecular cancer.","source_span":"Merkel cell carcinoma, also called neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin or trabecular cancer"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma starts most often in areas of skin exposed to the sun, especially the head and neck, as well as the arms, legs, and trunk.","source_span":"Merkel cell carcinoma starts most often in areas of skin exposed to the sun, especially the head and neck, as well as the arms, legs, and trunk."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma tends to grow quickly and metastasize at an early stage, usually spreading first to nearby lymph nodes and then to distant lymph nodes, skin, lungs, brain, bones, or other organs.","source_span":"Merkel cell carcinoma tends to grow quickly and to metastasize (spread) at an early stage. It usually spreads first to nearby lymph nodes and then may spread to lymph nodes or skin in distant parts of the body, lungs, brain, bones, or other organs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Risk factors for Merkel cell carcinoma include heavy natural sunlight exposure, artificial sunlight such as tanning beds or PUVA therapy for psoriasis, a weakened immune system (e.g. chronic lymphocytic leukemia or HIV infection), immunosuppressive drugs such as after an organ transplant, a history of other cancers, and being older than 50, male, or white.","source_span":"Risk factors for Merkel cell carcinoma include the following: - Being exposed to a lot of natural sunlight. - Being exposed to artificial sunlight, such as from tanning beds or psoralen and ultraviolet A (PUVA) therapy for psoriasis. - Having an immune system weakened by disease...- Taking drugs that make the immune system less active...- Having a history of other types of cancer. - Being older than 50 years, male, or white."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma usually appears as a single lump that is fast-growing, painless, firm and dome-shaped or raised, and red or violet in color.","source_span":"Merkel cell carcinoma usually appears on sun-exposed skin as a single lump that is: - Fast-growing. - Painless. - Firm and dome-shaped or raised. - Red or violet in color."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Merkel cell carcinoma is diagnosed using physical exam and history, a full-body skin exam, and a skin biopsy.","source_span":"Physical exam and history...Full-body skin exam...Skin biopsy: The removal of skin cells or tissues so they can be viewed under a microscope"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Prognosis for Merkel cell carcinoma depends on the stage of cancer, its location in the body, whether it is newly diagnosed or recurred, the patient's age and general health, and how deeply the tumor has grown into the skin.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The stage of the cancer...- Where the cancer is in the body. - Whether the cancer has just been diagnosed or has recurred...Prognosis also depends on how deeply the tumor has grown into the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"The stages used for Merkel cell carcinoma are Stage 0 (carcinoma in situ), IA, IB, IIA, IIB, IIC, IIIA, IIIB, and IV.","source_span":"Stage 0 (carcinoma in situ) Stage IA Stage IB Stage IIA Stage IIB Stage IIC Stage IIIA Stage IIIB Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","statement":"Staging tests for Merkel cell carcinoma may include CT scan, MRI, PET scan, and lymph node biopsy including sentinel lymph node biopsy.","source_span":"CT scan (CAT scan)...MRI (magnetic resonance imaging)...PET scan (positron emission tomography scan)...Lymph node biopsy: There are two main types of lymph node biopsy used to stage Merkel cell carcinoma...Sentinel lymph node biopsy"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[1],"question":"By what other names is Merkel cell carcinoma known?","answer":"Merkel cell carcinoma is also called neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin or trabecular cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which areas of the body does Merkel cell carcinoma most often start in?","answer":"It starts most often in sun-exposed areas of skin, especially the head and neck, as well as the arms, legs, and trunk."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How quickly does Merkel cell carcinoma tend to spread, and where does it spread first?","answer":"Merkel cell carcinoma tends to grow quickly and metastasize at an early stage, usually spreading first to nearby lymph nodes before possibly reaching distant lymph nodes, skin, lungs, brain, bones, or other organs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the risk factors for Merkel cell carcinoma?","answer":"Risk factors include heavy natural or artificial sunlight exposure (such as tanning beds or PUVA therapy for psoriasis), a weakened immune system from disease like chronic lymphocytic leukemia or HIV, immunosuppressive drugs such as after an organ transplant, a history of other cancers, and being older than 50, male, or white."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What does a typical Merkel cell carcinoma lump look like?","answer":"It usually appears as a single lump that is fast-growing, painless, firm and dome-shaped or raised, and red or violet in color."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_3","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What are the stages of Merkel cell carcinoma?","answer":"The stages are Stage 0 (carcinoma in situ), IA, IB, IIA, IIB, IIC, IIIA, IIIB, and IV."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the United States.","source_span":"Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the United States."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"The most common types of skin cancer are squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma, which are also called nonmelanoma skin cancers.","source_span":"The most common types of skin cancer are squamous cell carcinoma, which forms in the squamous cells and basal cell carcinoma, which forms in the basal cells. Squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma are also called nonmelanoma skin cancers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"Melanoma forms in the melanocytes and is a less common type of skin cancer that grows and spreads quickly.","source_span":"Melanoma, which forms in the melanocytes, is a less common type of skin cancer that grows and spreads quickly."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"Skin cancer can occur anywhere on the body but is most common in areas exposed to sunlight, such as the face, neck, hands, and arms.","source_span":"Skin cancer can occur anywhere on the body, but it is most common in areas exposed to sunlight, such as the face, neck, hands, and arms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing for at least 30 years.","source_span":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing for at least 30 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"The number of new cases of nonmelanoma skin cancer appears to be increasing every year, but these cancers can usually be cured.","source_span":"The number of new cases of nonmelanoma skin cancer appears to be increasing every year. These nonmelanoma skin cancers can usually be cured."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"Risk factors for nonmelanoma skin cancer include long-term natural or artificial sunlight exposure, fair complexion, having actinic keratosis, past radiation treatment, a weakened immune system, and exposure to arsenic.","source_span":"Risk factors for nonmelanoma skin cancer: - Being exposed to natural sunlight or artificial sunlight...- Having a fair complexion...- Having actinic keratosis. - Past treatment with radiation. - Having a weakened immune system. - Being exposed to arsenic."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","statement":"One study found that wearing sunscreen can help prevent actinic keratoses, scaly patches of skin that sometimes become squamous cell carcinoma.","source_span":"One study found that wearing sunscreen can help prevent actinic keratoses, scaly patches of skin that sometimes become squamous cell carcinoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Which cancer is the most common cancer in the United States?","answer":"Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the United States."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two most common types of skin cancer and what are they collectively called?","answer":"The two most common types are squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma, which are together called nonmelanoma skin cancers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Where does melanoma form and how does it behave compared to other skin cancers?","answer":"Melanoma forms in the melanocytes and is a less common type of skin cancer that grows and spreads quickly."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[3],"question":"On which parts of the body is skin cancer most likely to occur?","answer":"Skin cancer can occur anywhere on the body but is most common in areas exposed to sunlight, such as the face, neck, hands, and arms."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[4],"question":"For how long has the number of new melanoma cases been rising?","answer":"The number of new cases of melanoma has been increasing for at least 30 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000028_4","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the risk factors specifically associated with nonmelanoma skin cancer?","answer":"Risk factors include long-term natural or artificial sunlight exposure, fair complexion, having actinic keratosis, past radiation treatment, a weakened immune system, and exposure to arsenic."}
